WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.500
You have to get started.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.690
Once you get started, you'll continue.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:10.200
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here again, and today
I want to talk to you about my advice

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.180
for a first time language learner.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.790
Um, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, uh, click

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.320
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.520
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.300
So, uh, the reason I
want to talk about this.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.860
I have a brother, an older
brother, and he lives in Windsor,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.900
Ontario, not far from Toronto.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.040
I went to visit him.

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:39.540
He's kind of, you know,
bedbound, bedridden.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:45.440
Uh, so I want to go there and I wanted
to go there and cheer him up and give

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.310
him things to do because he's in the bed.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:52.365
Like he, he does exercises to try get, you
know, strengthen his muscles, but for a

00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:55.815
variety of reasons that I won't get into,
it's going to be a bit of a chore for him.

00:00:56.025 --> 00:00:58.425
He's going to, it's going to take
him a while before he gradually

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.720
gets, you know, more mobile.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.610
So I said, okay, you know, kind
of get the brain moving as well.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.210
So I bought him an iPad and I said, first
of all, you should do Scrabble because

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.130
as kids we used to love playing Scrabble.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.170
And the great thing about doing Scrabble
on the iPad, which my wife does, I don't

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.789
do it, is that the other person like
you're playing against the computer,

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.720
the computer puts a word out right away.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.035
So you don't have to wait.

00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:29.165
The worst thing in Scrabble to me was I
was fairly quick at putting out a word.

00:01:29.195 --> 00:01:32.795
And then if were, if I had one opponent,
opponent, or two opponents, it would

00:01:32.795 --> 00:01:34.805
take forever before I had I turn again.

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:38.105
Whereas if you play with the computer
it's instant, which is kind of fun.

00:01:38.315 --> 00:01:43.085
So I got my brother onto Scrabble and I
also said you should learn a language,

00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:44.135
which language do you want to learn?

00:01:44.165 --> 00:01:44.705
Spanish.

00:01:44.735 --> 00:01:45.185
Okay.

00:01:45.455 --> 00:01:47.345
Get on LingQ and learn Spanish.

00:01:47.915 --> 00:01:50.495
So, okay whatever he wasn't
tremendously motivated.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.240
But at some level, he would
like to learn Spanish.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.970
He does speak French, you
know, grew up in Montreal.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:02.280
And, uh, so, uh, anyway, he calls me
the other day and he says, Steve, I

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.539
have a real problem in using LingQ.

00:02:06.449 --> 00:02:07.059
Okay.

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:10.169
So I thought maybe it was something
with our functionality and so forth.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.190
And he said, uh, no, it's like
all these words, I forget them.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.400
So the Spanish words, as soon as I learn
something, look it up, I've forgotten it.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:27.734
And I thought to myself, you
know, how many people uh, become

00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:31.304
disappointed or frustrated because
they can't remember things.

00:02:31.905 --> 00:02:35.535
Uh, to me, it's obvious that
I'm not going to remember.

00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:40.334
I have to see the words, um, 10
times, and then there are words that I

00:02:40.334 --> 00:02:43.995
know, and then I'll, then I'll forget
them again in Persian or Arabic.

00:02:44.505 --> 00:02:49.515
Uh, so I thought to myself, you know,
there are so many people who at some

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:51.945
level would like to learn a language.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.850
And, you know, they
just never get anywhere.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.200
So these are not the keeners, the people
who know what they want to do in language

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.520
who say understand LingQ or who like
LingQ or don't like LingQ, but they

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.700
know that they want to learn language
and they know what they have to do.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.980
But the vast majority of people don't.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.030
The vast majority of people may even
go to a bookstore and buy a book on

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.950
language learning and never do much.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.859
So I would like to suggest three bits
of advice for someone who is not kind

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:27.400
of, uh, an experienced language or may
be trying to learn their first language.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.530
The first thing is just get started.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.100
There are expressions that
exist in many languages.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.410
The gist of which is the first
step is the job half done.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.680
You know, it doesn't matter
whether it's, you know, I'm right

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.570
now, I'm power washing my deck.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:45.900
Okay.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.980
You have to get started once you
get started, you'll continue.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.970
I got to finish the deck, but as
long as I look at it and don't do

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.670
anything it's not going to happen.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.040
So the first thing is if
you'll just get started.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.470
Just get started.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.820
And I thought again with my brother,
I finally got him to get started.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:06.480
Okay.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.320
Get started.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.620
The second thing is try to create a habit.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.220
I mean, we all know that if we
haven't been exercising for a while

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.940
and then we go to the gym, the first
two or three sessions are difficult.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:23.640
But if we are able to create a habit
where we go to the gym regularly, three

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.349
times a week, four times a week, We
start to feel unhappy if we haven't

00:04:28.349 --> 00:04:32.400
had that exercise because we've created
a habit at different levels, both

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:38.130
psychologically and physically, we've
developed this, this, uh, this dependence,

00:04:38.219 --> 00:04:40.890
this need for that regular activity.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.900
And I feel that way about
my language learning.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:49.185
If I let too many days go by where I,
you know, spent time with the language

00:04:49.185 --> 00:04:51.164
that I'm learning, it bothers me.

00:04:51.164 --> 00:04:56.145
So I have that habit for better or for
worse, but if you can, create a habit

00:04:56.385 --> 00:04:58.305
that will drive you to keep going.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:03.465
So first of all, get started,
second of all, try to create

00:05:03.465 --> 00:05:05.685
a habit to do certain things.

00:05:05.685 --> 00:05:09.015
That's why we have so many things at
LingQ, like, you know, maintaining

00:05:09.015 --> 00:05:12.855
your streak or following your
statistics because sometimes you

00:05:12.855 --> 00:05:17.940
need that, you know, a little nudge
all the time to keep you going.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.620
Because if you put in the
time, you will improve.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:27.900
That, that gets back to the statement
that I heard from this director of the

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.580
San Diego University Language Department
who said there's only two things that

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.300
matter in language learning: the attitude
of the learner and the time you put in.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.270
You've got to put in the time.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.380
You've got to keep going.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.980
So whatever it takes to develop
a habit so that you keep going.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.440
The third thing is, and if I expand from,
you know, the attitude of the learner

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.680
and the time you put in to this formula
from Kato Lomb or Lomb Kato Hungarian

00:05:55.890 --> 00:06:01.440
polyglot, uh, she had this equation,
which basically was sort of the time

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:10.575
you put in and the attitude divided by
negativity, frustration, inhibition,

00:06:10.725 --> 00:06:12.975
fear, all of the negative stuff.

00:06:13.245 --> 00:06:16.845
So you have to put in the time,
you have to have a positive

00:06:16.845 --> 00:06:18.675
attitude, you have to want to learn.

00:06:18.765 --> 00:06:23.085
You have to think you can learn, but
you have to minimize the negative stuff.

00:06:23.265 --> 00:06:28.965
So if you forget, that's normal,
don't expect to remember.

00:06:29.325 --> 00:06:33.795
Recognize that if you keep putting the
time, putting the time in, if you keep

00:06:34.125 --> 00:06:37.365
a positive attitude, you will learn.

00:06:37.545 --> 00:06:41.835
And I think very often the beginner
language learner thinks that they have

00:06:41.835 --> 00:06:45.765
to learn these rules and if they don't
remember the rules, then they won't learn

00:06:45.765 --> 00:06:49.845
the language or they've been at it for
several months and they still can't speak.

00:06:50.055 --> 00:06:52.155
And so they, this negativity comes in.

00:06:52.155 --> 00:06:53.265
I still can't do anything.

00:06:53.265 --> 00:06:53.955
I'm no good.

00:06:54.544 --> 00:06:59.190
And yet, you know, the, the beginner
language learner has to understand

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:03.270
that with enough exposure to the
language, if you can develop a habit...

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.720
first of all, you have to get started
and then you develop this habit that

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.890
you are regularly listening and reading
to and do whatever you'd like to do.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:15.120
If it's Duolingo, I'm not going
to, you know, personally, I prefer

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:19.590
working with LingQ, but as I've said
many times, you know, people should

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.260
do what they want to do so that
they enjoy it, but stay with it.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:24.599
Keep doing it.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.320
And when you encounter
difficulties and I think...

00:07:28.349 --> 00:07:30.960
I keep saying it, and yet it's so
important for beginner language

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.320
learners to realize you get
it wrong, it doesn't matter.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.059
You can't remember, it doesn't matter.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.369
You don't understand, it doesn't matter.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.130
Nothing matters except the fact that
you maintain a positive attitude,

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.059
believe that you will eventually get
there and keep on putting in the time.

00:07:49.799 --> 00:07:55.080
And in order to keep on putting in
the time, you may have to set up some

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.159
routine that helps you create this habit.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:05.219
So this is kind of what I told my
brother and I thought to myself, you

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:09.450
know, it's not just my brother, it's
many people who never, who at some

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:15.120
level would like to be, uh, fluent in
another language would like to be good

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.810
language learners think they aren't
good language learners and therefore

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.270
they end up not achieving very much.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:26.610
So I hope that's helpful to those of
you, and maybe people listened to me

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.850
are all good language learners, but
maybe some of your friends are not.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.140
So I hope that's helpful.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:31.770
Yeah.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.850
And oh, and I think I'll leave you with
two videos, uh, from, uh, before where I

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.730
talk about being a lazy language learner.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:46.620
And I also talk about the three
keys to language learning success.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.000
Thanks for listening.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:48.530
Bye for now.

